ok takes the half-open f-file, aiming at the bishop on f5.","The next best move, 11…O-O, would drop Black's winning chances from 51% to 26% (+2.88).","11…Rf8 wins a bishop for two pawns, picking it up with 13…hxg6.","Sets up a sequence and wins a bishop for two pawns once the captures settle, picking it up with 13…hxg6.","White answers 12.Qh5+, forking the king and the pawn."]},{"ply":25,"name":"13.Bxg6+","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"13.Bxg6+!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the bishop on g6 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 13…hxg6 14.Qxg6+ Kd7 15.d3 the engine rates it −0.08.","Takes the pawn on g6.","Protects the queen on h5, which was hanging.","13.Bxg6+ protects the queen on h5 and lines up bishop and queen on the diagonal.","Lines up bishop and queen on the diagonal, so the two pieces push in the same direction."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"13.Bxg6+!! gives up the bishop on g6 to stay in the game: after it White holds the balance (−0.08); anything else leaves White clearly worse (−2.80)."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"13.Bxg6+ puts the bishop on g6 where it can be taken by the h7 pawn. That's a bishop on offer."},{"title":"If Black takes the bishop","text":"13…hxg6 is met by 14.Qxg6+, taking the g6 pawn with check. Then 16.Bd2 threatens Bxa5+, winning a knight, and 17.Nf7 forks the queen and the pawn. White is a pawn down: White holds the balance (−0.01). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 13.Qe2, is clearly weaker (−2.80 against −0.08). Black answers 13…Rxf5, taking the bishop on f5. That would leave White clearly worse."}]},{"ply":35,"name":"18.Qa4","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"18.Qa4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Adds a decisive attacker on the knight on a5: 2 attackers against 1 defender.","The next best move, 18.Na3, would 